From b7ee47cc6a71a464b79673331573171917f4cd41 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 22:37:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf(container): keep a ThemeOverride's scope while its theme
 is the same

Applying an override reset the SVG and text-metric caches of the whole app
for EACH widget it walked (internal/cache/theme.go), and every
ThemeOverride.Refresh walked the content again with a new scope, so a new
cache ID: a themed list paid for both on every refresh, and every row a
list created under it reset the caches once more.

- cache.NewOverrideScope resets the theme caches once, when a scope is
  created; OverrideScope.Apply walks a tree without touching them.
  OverrideTheme keeps its behaviour (a new scope per call).
- ThemeOverride keeps its scope while Theme is the same value and creates a
  new one (new cache ID, one reset) when another theme is assigned. Theme
  values of a type that cannot be compared get a new scope each time, as
  before. A theme changed in place keeps its scope; assign a new value for
  its icons to be rendered again.
- Objects that already belong to the scope are not stored again: re-applying
  a scope to 800 objects went from 800 allocations to none.

Measured in the Sytue suite with the uncommitted fork in a go.work, a Mail
inbox Refresh (50 and 2000 threads): -15-21 % wall and -22-25 % CPU per
refresh, allocations 3950 -> 2412 (-39 %) and bytes -49 %; the calendar
month and week paint, -10-29 % with 6-19 % fewer allocations. New tests
show applying a scope keeps the caches and that assigning another theme
still gives a new scope and renders the icons in its colours.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
